  "body": "Fork synced at v0.18.0. Not stacked: branched from upstream main (see \"Subplate\" below).\n\n## What changed\nThe index-swing clause judged only the tool against the work the rotary sweeps. It now has a second half, `crates/app/src/posting_swing_standing.rs`, which judges the swept work (stock, parts, the fixtures that turn with it) against what stands still:\n- **The face the rotary stands on.** The machine file has a new optional key on a work-turning rotary, `axis_to_table`: the rotary's centre height over its own mounting face. `crates/post/src/machine/swing.rs` reads it by the same law as `nose_to_axis`: unstated reads as unstated, garbled input is refused by name. The microARC 4 states 3.15 in (Tormach 4th-axis comparison sheet). If the swept radius plus 5 mm reaches that face, the post is refused, and the message names the op, the index (`A90.000`), the swept radius, the figure and the clearance.\n- **Fixed fixtures over the work's X span.** Each is judged by its bounding box's distance from the A line in YZ. A strike is refused as \"standing fixture <name>\". Steel that sits wholly beside the work in X is clear.\n- **What it cannot judge is named, never silent.** A machine with no `axis_to_table`, a fixed fixture with no facets or non-finite facets, and a fixed fixture whose box reaches across the centerline over the work (a tailstock, the rotary's own body) are listed in the gate's not-judged line: `index swing did not judge the work against the standing steel: ...`. That line reaches the status line, the viewer header and the script result through `GateReport::not_judged`.\n- `posting_index_swing.rs`: `swept_radius` becomes `swept` (it returns the radius plus the X span). `work_a` and `index_angle` are now shared with the new clause. The tool half's behaviour is unchanged.\n- The microARC tester-kit project carries the new figure (80.01 mm). `assets/machines/AGENTS.md` documents the key.\n- Decision file: `docs/decisions/1107-swing-work-vs-standing.md`.\n\n**Subplate (#1082).** Not stacked on #1082. A table, plate or subplate under the rotary raises the centerline along with the rotary, so the nearest standing face under the rotary is always its own centre height below the line, whatever the stack. #1082's subplate is covered by this law without reading its stack model. Steel beside the rotary that reaches under the work is judged once it is modelled as a fixed fixture.\n\n## How tested\nPinning tests in `crates/app/tests/index_swing.rs` (13 pass: 9 existing, unchanged, plus 4 new):\n- `work_swinging_into_the_face_the_rotary_stands_on_is_refused_by_name`: the bar on a rotary 2 mm inside the margin is refused by name and the post writes nothing. The same bar with 2 mm to spare passes.\n- `a_standing_fixture_under_the_work_is_judged_by_its_distance_from_the_line`: a low plate under the bar clears, a block beside the bar in X clears, and a riser 15 mm under the line is refused by name.\n- `standing_steel_the_swing_cannot_read_is_named_not_judged`: with no `axis_to_table` the program posts and the gap is named. A tailstock across the line is named too.\n- `the_shipped_microarc_states_its_centre_height_over_the_table`.\n- Post unit test `nose_to_axis_reads_scaled_or_refuses_by_name` is extended to `axis_to_table`.\n\nGate: `cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` on the head 5716d312: green (post suites and 98 app suites plus lib, 4.1 min, 0 failed). `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-post --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n\n## Not exercised\n- The GUI by hand.\n- A real long part on hardware.\n- Per-angle swings: the swing is judged over the full turn, like the tool half.\n- Fixtures that are not boxes are judged by their bounding box, which is conservative.\n- The release lanes.\n\nChangelog: The index swing now refuses a post when the work it sweeps would strike the face the rotary stands on or a standing fixture; machine files state the rotary's centre height as `axis_to_table` (microARC 4: 3.15 in), and steel it cannot judge is named (#1107)\n\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #1107\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
